  {
    "episode_id": 84466,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84466.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 49,
    "turn_text": "Well, it's going to be received well by me because I've been working on ag immigration reform bill for a number of years. There's been four of us, four Democrats, excuse me, eight of us, four Democrats, four Republicans. We passed a bill twice in the house, almost had it done last year and attached to the omnibus bill. But we came up just a bit short. So we're going to reintroduce it this year. And what we're trying to do is create a legal workforce for agriculture. There are so many undocumented people working in agriculture today across the country, not just in Idaho, but across the country. And they've been here for many of them, 10, 20 years, producing food for us. The H2A program is broken. We're trying to reform that. We're trying to bring these people out of the woodwork, give them permanent green card status so that they can come and go. They can work in agriculture. They have to stay employed in agriculture. And it's a good bill, worked on between Republicans and Democrats. But it needs to be done because the biggest challenge right now, if you go ask any agriculture producer in this country, what's your biggest issue? Workforce. And you could just deport all these people if you wanted to. And some people have suggested that. How much are you willing to pay for a gallon of milk? 15, 20 bucks.",
